In a previous game, I was offered the tournament challenge (can't use 
Elbereth until you retrieve it from the "Kingdom of Loathing") and 
accepted, thinking that the Kingdom of Loathing was an extra side-
dungeon. This was a mistake which cost a number of promising wizards.

This game, jimrandomh the gnomish wizard started with a spellbook of 
scare monster, which for most of the game served as a crude but 
essential Elbereth substitute. I went down the main dungeon for a bit, 
then backtracked and went into the mines (which are usually fatal). 
Along the way, I found a magic lamp; and minetown yielded another! 
However, neither was blessed and the only altar was the cross-aligned 
minetown one, so I headed back up and went for Sokoban, which yielded a 
bag of holding.

It wasn't until level 14 or so that I finally found an altar, and I was 
surviving by the skin of my teeth the whole time. When I DID find an 
altar, it was below bigroom, which I had bypassed with a wand of 
digging. I then realized I had no water, and had to dilute potions in 
the bigroom's fountains. (Thank Thoth for invisibility!) First wish was 
for +3 SDSM (got +3); second was for the Orb of Fate (had a 2/3 chance 
but didn't get it).

Normally, when I reach this stage with a wizard I start playing ultra-
conservatively: I spend about 30k turns polypiling and alchemizing 
potions, dancing with foocubi, etc. to get my max HP up, collect a 
best-theoretically-possible ascenscion kit, and so on. This game, I 
decided to play more quickly for a change. I finished up the quest, 
then the mines, then castle, stopped briefly to polypile scrolls, 
amulets and rings (I had a +oPoly), then hit the castle. Gehennom went 
quickly; with magic mapping and detect treasure spells I was never 
tempted to dwell on any levels. (The castle wand, by the way, was 0:1; 
I used one for speed boots and never used the rest.)

The planes, of course, gave me ample reminder of why I prefer to play 
slowly. I used one "oLS on air, and another on the astral plane. I 
defeated Death, then used a helm of opposite alignment to match my 
alignment to his altar and ascend.

Whew. Next up, a valkyrie for the Birdie, hopefully with bells on.

